Clamp filter taps per axis at a region edge in the super-sampled texture fetch

The super-sampled fetch (TEX_PER_SAMPLE_BIT, filtered through the phase LUT)
dropped every filter tap but the first when the read's texel lay on any edge
of the clamp region (CLAMP / REGION_CLAMP), in both directions. A game that
copies a picture in clamped strips (DW5XL's menus: 64-column strips, each
REGION_CLAMPed to its own texels, 448 lines squeezed into 256) then lost the
vertical filtering in each strip's first and last column: horizontal edges
snapped to the nearest source line, a dark notch at every strip boundary
(2x, and 1x with 0004). The GS clamps a filtered read one axis at a time, so
keep the filter and move only the taps that would leave the region back to
the edge texel, along the clamped axis. Taps never leave the region, and
reads away from a region edge are unchanged. Nearest magnification is
unchanged.
Shader bytecode for the four ubershader variants (FEEDBACK_COLOR x
FEEDBACK_DEPTH) compiled with Granite's pinned glslang/shaderc (submodules
glslang 8f79344b, shaderc 42c364eb, SPIRV-Tools 556c7ca9, SPIRV-Headers
00898b20; the unchanged shader rebuilt with it is byte-identical to the
shipped bytecode), glslc -O --target-env=vulkan1.1 --target-spv=spv1.3
-DSTAGE_COMPUTE=1, spliced with tools/parallel_gs_splice_shader.py.

Applies on top of 0001, 0002, 0003 and 0004 (DW5XL issue 009 v4.3).

